course: dictionary
lesson: 11905
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them tell you which meaning to use. Look for clues, then ask: which meaning fits here? «Just bung it in the bag» tells you bung means to put something somewhere quickly, because you put things in bags.
explain: The same word can name a thing. «Dad pulled the bung out of the barrel» shows that a bung can be a stopper that closes a hole. Here, the barrel is the clue: a barrel has a hole that needs a stopper.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «My nose is all bunged up» means your nose is blocked, because a blocked nose makes it hard to breathe. Swap in blocked, and it still makes sense.
fact: Bung can be a noun (a bung) or a verb (to bung).
fact: As a noun, «bung» means a stopper or plug that closes a hole in a barrel, bottle or sink.
fact: As a verb, «bung» means to put or throw something somewhere quickly and carelessly.
fact: «Bunged up» means blocked, as in «my nose is all bunged up».
fact: In British slang, a «bung» is a bribe: secret money given to get an unfair advantage. This is cheating.
exercise: «Just bung your bag in the car.» What does bung mean here? | To pay someone secretly | To break and stop working | To block a hole with a stopper | * To put something somewhere quickly and carelessly
exercise: «The barrel had a wooden bung in the top.» What is a bung? | A kind of drink | A small bird | A loud noise | * A stopper that closes a hole
exercise: «My nose is all bunged up.» What does bunged up mean? | Shiny and bright | * Blocked, so air cannot get through easily | Full of jokes | Sneezing a lot
test: «Our old printer has gone bung.» What does gone bung mean? | Turned very quiet | Bought a new one | * Stopped working or broke down | Lost its paper tray
test: «Someone tried to bung the referee with money.» What is a bung in this sentence? | * A secret bribe, to get an unfair advantage | A bag of sweets | A loud whistle | A new football
exam: Which sentence uses bung as a noun? | * «The barrel has a wooden bung in the top.» | «Bung the lid on the jar.» | «Please bung your shoes by the door.» | «My nose is bunged up.»
